{"cases":[{"contract":"Different base seeds produce diversity in at least one eligible controlled choice while each seed remains exactly replayable.","disposition":"preserved","id":"different-seed-diversity","requirements":[{"backends":["in_process","process"],"fidelity":"simulation_model"}],"sources":[{"path":"tools/gomad2/nemesis/meta_test.go","tests":["TestMetaSeeds"]}],"stage":"SIM-4","status":"implemented"},{"contract":"Crash-state exploration enumerates every valid state for bounded small operation sets lazily, without duplicates, and can resume from a checkpoint.","disposition":"replaced","id":"enumerate-crash-states","replacement":"V2 test loops demonstrate expected small crash states but do not define a bounded resumable enumeration identity; v3 requires canonical state identities, explicit bounds, and resumable checkpoints.","requirements":[{"backends":["in_process","process"],"fidelity":"simulation_model"}],"sources":[{"path":"tools/gomad2/internal/tests/behavior/disk_crash_test.go","tests":["TestCrashDiskMultipleFileMultipleWrite","TestCrashDiskSingleFileMultipleWrite","TestCrashDiskTwoFiles"]}],"stage":"SIM-3","status":"implemented"},{"contract":"File sync persists file data and metadata dependencies, while directory sync separately persists namespace entries and parent-directory dependencies.","disposition":"replaced","id":"file-directory-sync","replacement":"V2 contains questioned directory-sync expectations; v3 records file and directory durability as separate explicit dependency closures and rejects ambiguous host-filesystem inference.","requirements":[{"backends":["in_process","process"],"fidelity":"simulation_model"}],"sources":[{"path":"tools/gomad2/internal/tests/behavior/disk_crash_test.go","tests":["TestCrashDiskFileSync","TestCrashDiskWriteRenameSyncDir","TestCrashDiskWriteRenameSyncFile"]},{"path":"tools/gomad2/internal/tests/behavior/disk_test.go","tests":["TestDiskBasic","TestDiskSyncDir"]}],"stage":"SIM-3","status":"implemented"},{"contract":"Every directed link applies its configured deterministic delay to delivery and records that delay in replayable network transitions.","disposition":"preserved","id":"fixed-link-latency","requirements":[{"backends":["in_process","process"],"fidelity":"simulation_model"}],"sources":[{"path":"tools/gomad2/examples/machines_test.go","tests":["TestMachines"]}],"stage":"SIM-2","status":"implemented"},{"contract":"Graceful stop follows explicit shutdown semantics; harsh crash revokes the exact incarnation and deterministically breaks its established and pending TCP operations.","disposition":"replaced","id":"graceful-stop-vs-crash-connection","replacement":"V2 observes broken connections but does not bind all in-flight packets to an incarnation; v3 rejects delivery from a crashed incarnation and distinguishes stop from crash in lifecycle records.","requirements":[{"backends":["process"],"fidelity":"hard_isolation"},{"backends":["in_process","process"],"fidelity":"simulation_model"}],"sources":[{"path":"tools/gomad2/internal/tests/behavior/crash_test.go","tests":["TestCrashClientTCPConnBreaks","TestCrashServerTCPConnBreaks"]}],"stage":"SIM-2","status":"implemented"},{"contract":"Each node has a stable identity and address across lifecycle transitions, and every start or restart creates a distinct monotonically increasing incarnation handle.","disposition":"replaced","id":"independent-node-identity-lifecycle","replacement":"V2 exposes machine pointers and translator-era lifecycle behavior; v3 uses stable serializable node identities and explicit incarnation handles so stale work can be rejected.","requirements":[{"backends":["in_process","process"],"fidelity":"simulation_model"}],"sources":[{"path":"tools/gomad2/internal/tests/behavior/machine_test.go","tests":["TestMachineCrash","TestMachineCurrent","TestMachineGo","TestMachineLabel"]}],"stage":"SIM-1","status":"implemented"},{"contract":"A bounded deterministic nemesis can repeatedly partition and heal links and crash or restart selected node incarnations with every action recorded for exact replay.","disposition":"replaced","id":"nemesis-partition-restart","replacement":"V2 combines random nemesis behavior with implicit machine state; v3 requires bounded action plans, stable target identities, explicit eligible alternatives, and exact replay records.","requirements":[{"backends":["process"],"fidelity":"hard_isolation"},{"backends":["in_process","process"],"fidelity":"simulation_model"}],"sources":[{"path":"tools/gomad2/nemesis/nemesis_test.go","tests":["TestPartition"]}],"stage":"SIM-4","status":"implemented"},{"contract":"A crash selects only a dependency-valid subset of pending durable operations, applies it atomically, and records the selected canonical persistence state.","disposition":"preserved","id":"partial-crash-persistence","requirements":[{"backends":["in_process","process"],"fidelity":"simulation_model"}],"sources":[{"path":"tools/gomad2/internal/tests/behavior/crash_test.go","tests":["TestCrashRestartFilesystemPartialInner"]},{"path":"tools/gomad2/internal/tests/behavior/disk_crash_test.go","tests":["TestCrashDiskBasic","TestCrashDiskMultipleFileMultipleWrite","TestCrashDiskSingleFileMultipleWrite"]}],"stage":"SIM-3","status":"implemented"},{"contract":"Directional partition causes deterministic timeout or refusal according to connection phase; healing permits a new connection without reviving stale incarnation traffic.","disposition":"replaced","id":"partition-timeout-heal-reconnect","replacement":"V2 connectivity leaves symmetry and unreachable-versus-rejected outcomes implicit; v3 models directional links, connection phase, deadlines, and heal/reconnect transitions explicitly.","requirements":[{"backends":["in_process","process"],"fidelity":"simulation_model"}],"sources":[{"path":"tools/gomad2/internal/tests/behavior/net_test.go","tests":["TestNetTcpDisconnectBasic"]},{"path":"tools/gomad2/nemesis/nemesis_test.go","tests":["TestPartition"]}],"stage":"SIM-2","status":"implemented"},{"contract":"Rename and truncate contribute explicit namespace, file-content, file-metadata, and directory dependencies to the set of valid post-crash states.","disposition":"replaced","id":"rename-truncate-crash-dependencies","replacement":"V2 enumerates examples but leaves some rename and directory-sync outcomes as questioned expectations; v3 derives all valid outcomes from explicit operation dependencies.","requirements":[{"backends":["in_process","process"],"fidelity":"simulation_model"}],"sources":[{"path":"tools/gomad2/internal/tests/behavior/disk_crash_test.go","tests":["TestCrashDiskWriteRename","TestCrashDiskWriteRenameSyncDir","TestCrashDiskWriteRenameSyncFile","TestCrashDiskWriteRenameSyncFileAndDir","TestCrashDiskWriteRenameTwiceSyncFile"]},{"path":"tools/gomad2/internal/tests/behavior/disk_test.go","tests":["TestDiskTruncateGrow","TestDiskTruncateShrink"]}],"stage":"SIM-3","status":"implemented"},{"contract":"Restart preserves only acknowledged durable volume state, recreates model-owned volatile node state, retains stable node identity and address, and increments incarnation.","disposition":"replaced","id":"restart-durable-and-volatile","replacement":"V2 restarts translated functions in one process and does not prove hard isolation or in-flight packet fate; v3 separates modeled durability from model-owned volatile state, binds traffic to incarnations, and reserves fresh package globals for process-backed hard isolation.","requirements":[{"backends":["process"],"fidelity":"hard_isolation"},{"backends":["in_process","process"],"fidelity":"simulation_model"}],"sources":[{"path":"tools/gomad2/internal/tests/behavior/crash_test.go","tests":["TestCrashRestartGlobals","TestCrashRestartNetwork","TestCrashRestartPendingDisk"]}],"stage":"SIM-1","status":"implemented"},{"contract":"Repeated execution with the same seed, spec, backend identity, and inputs produces equal detached transition and outcome identities.","disposition":"preserved","id"